{"body": "## What the actual construct adds, and where the accounting slips\n\nThe construct does real work: it separates the *target* of P9's compatibility objection from the *locus* at which a repair would be made, and it flags a strongly deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") as a competitor rather than absorbing it. Those are additions beyond the packet, and they are marked as additions. But the accountant's ledger in the construct — \"what the source says\" versus \"what I add\" — contains at least one entry that is misattributed, and it is the entry on which the central move rests.\n\n### The misattribution\n\nThe construct says (§2):\n\n> \"Calling that a defect of the rule mislocates the criticism, in exactly the way P7 warns against. It is the same error as saying 'higher revision always supersedes' is refuted by a record with a corrupt high revision: the rule was never the thing under attack.\"\n\nThis presents the analogy as licensed by P7. P7 does not say that misdirected criticism *is* the error it lists; it says the targets *differ*:\n\n> \"A criticism of the rule 'higher revision always supersedes' is not the same as a criticism of a record that carries a high revision but an implausible quantity. Preserving that distinction matters because one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.\"\n\nP7 thus marks a *distinction between targets* and the coherence of the four accept/reject combinations. It does not supply a warning against *mislocation*, nor does it claim that anyone who criticizes the rule on the strength of a corrupt record has made an error. The construct's \"in exactly the way P7 warns against\" attributes a normative claim (mislocation is an error) to a passage that supplies only a descriptive one (targets differ; combinations are coherent). That is an unsupported relationship between the two frozen passages — the kind of addition the question asked to be distinguished from what the source says.\n\nThe construct later acknowledges that P7 \"does not carry the locus information\" and that the locus/target distinction is its own addition (§4, §7). But §2's analogy rides on the same distinction *as if* it were already source-licensed. There is a tension: §4 says the target/locus distinction is mine; §2 uses it to explain what P7 \"warns against.\" Both cannot be the accounting.\n\n### A case that distinguishes the accounts\n\nConsider the arithmetic reading's defender, who says: the aggregation function is the account, and an objection that the function's inputs are incomplete is an objection to the function-as-account. Call this the *holist* position.\n\nUnder the construct's reading, the holist is mislocating — committing \"the same error as\" the corrupt-record critic. Under a reading that takes P7 literally, the holist is not committing an error at all: she rejects an account (a rule plus its data interface), while accepting the records. P7 explicitly says that combination is coherent. So the construct's relocation depends on treating a *coherent* combination as an *error*, in order to secure the analogy to P7. That is a substantive loss: the four accept/reject positions P7 enumerates become, under the construct, graded for locational correctness — a move P7's text does not authorize.\n\nI do not claim this refutes the construct's relocation. The relocation can stand on its own merits as a proposed re-description. The defect is narrower: the construct overstates the source's support at the one point where it needs the reader to accept that a competing reading is *misguided* rather than merely alternative.\n\n### A second, more uncertain worry\n\nThe construct's §2 says the arithmetic is \"correct *relative to its inputs*.\" That phrasing imports a soundness notion that the frozen material does not specify: P9 says the min/max bounds \"give an interval\" and that intervals \"lose correlations.\" Whether an interval that loses correlations is \"sound but incomplete\" or \"silent on a relevant dimension\" is not settled by the source, and the construct's choice of \"sound\" quietly does work in the argument by making the aggregation rule look like an innocent local computation. I flag this as a proposed defect rather than an established one, because \"correct relative to its inputs\" can be read as a plain statement about what min/max does, not a technical soundness claim.\n\n### What remains admissible\n\nThe construct's §5 does the honest thing: it preserves the deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") and the interval-as-representation reading as competitors to its own relocation. Those rival accounts survive the criticism above; the only thing weakened is §2's claim that the relocation is *the* P7-shaped reading. It is *a* P7-shaped reading, added by the construct, not licensed by P7's text as the corrective to a prior mislocation.\n\nThe discriminating question in §6 remains the right one, and the answer to it — whether the source ever treats bounds as an answer, a provisional summary, or only an example — is not in the material. That uncertainty should be retained. So should the three-way disambiguation; nothing in the misattribution defeats it, because the disambiguation does not depend on the analogy that P7 supplies a mislocation warning.", "commitments": "## What the actual construct adds, and where the accounting slips\n\nThe construct does real work: it separates the *target* of P9's compatibility objection from the *locus* at which a repair would be made, and it flags a strongly deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") as a competitor rather than absorbing it. Those are additions beyond the packet, and they are marked as additions. But the accountant's ledger in the construct — \"what the source says\" versus \"what I add\" — contains at least one entry that is misattributed, and it is the entry on which the central move rests.\n\n### The misattribution\n\nThe construct says (§2):\n\n> \"Calling that a defect of the rule mislocates the criticism, in exactly the way P7 warns against. It is the same error as saying 'higher revision always supersedes' is refuted by a record with a corrupt high revision: the rule was never the thing under attack.\"\n\nThis presents the analogy as licensed by P7. P7 does not say that misdirected criticism *is* the error it lists; it says the targets *differ*:\n\n> \"A criticism of the rule 'higher revision always supersedes' is not the same as a criticism of a record that carries a high revision but an implausible quantity. Preserving that distinction matters because one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.\"\n\nP7 thus marks a *distinction between targets* and the coherence of the four accept/reject combinations. It does not supply a warning against *mislocation*, nor does it claim that anyone who criticizes the rule on the strength of a corrupt record has made an error. The construct's \"in exactly the way P7 warns against\" attributes a normative claim (mislocation is an error) to a passage that supplies only a descriptive one (targets differ; combinations are coherent). That is an unsupported relationship between the two frozen passages — the kind of addition the question asked to be distinguished from what the source says.\n\nThe construct later acknowledges that P7 \"does not carry the locus information\" and that the locus/target distinction is its own addition (§4, §7). But §2's analogy rides on the same distinction *as if* it were already source-licensed. There is a tension: §4 says the target/locus distinction is mine; §2 uses it to explain what P7 \"warns against.\" Both cannot be the accounting.\n\n### A case that distinguishes the accounts\n\nConsider the arithmetic reading's defender, who says: the aggregation function is the account, and an objection that the function's inputs are incomplete is an objection to the function-as-account. Call this the *holist* position.\n\nUnder the construct's reading, the holist is mislocating — committing \"the same error as\" the corrupt-record critic. Under a reading that takes P7 literally, the holist is not committing an error at all: she rejects an account (a rule plus its data interface), while accepting the records. P7 explicitly says that combination is coherent. So the construct's relocation depends on treating a *coherent* combination as an *error*, in order to secure the analogy to P7. That is a substantive loss: the four accept/reject positions P7 enumerates become, under the construct, graded for locational correctness — a move P7's text does not authorize.\n\nI do not claim this refutes the construct's relocation. The relocation can stand on its own merits as a proposed re-description. The defect is narrower: the construct overstates the source's support at the one point where it needs the reader to accept that a competing reading is *misguided* rather than merely alternative.\n\n### A second, more uncertain worry\n\nThe construct's §2 says the arithmetic is \"correct *relative to its inputs*.\" That phrasing imports a soundness notion that the frozen material does not specify: P9 says the min/max bounds \"give an interval\" and that intervals \"lose correlations.\" Whether an interval that loses correlations is \"sound but incomplete\" or \"silent on a relevant dimension\" is not settled by the source, and the construct's choice of \"sound\" quietly does work in the argument by making the aggregation rule look like an innocent local computation. I flag this as a proposed defect rather than an established one, because \"correct relative to its inputs\" can be read as a plain statement about what min/max does, not a technical soundness claim.\n\n### What remains admissible\n\nThe construct's §5 does the honest thing: it preserves the deflationary reading (\"no change until evidence\") and the interval-as-representation reading as competitors to its own relocation. Those rival accounts survive the criticism above; the only thing weakened is §2's claim that the relocation is *the* P7-shaped reading. It is *a* P7-shaped reading, added by the construct, not licensed by P7's text as the corrective to a prior mislocation.\n\nThe discriminating question in §6 remains the right one, and the answer to it — whether the source ever treats bounds as an answer, a provisional summary, or only an example — is not in the material. That uncertainty should be retained. So should the three-way disambiguation; nothing in the misattribution defeats it, because the disambiguation does not depend on the analogy that P7 supplies a mislocation warning."}